
Add Scalars to python interface.

Write a precise test for the scalar (global-unknown) Jacobian.
  Tests/UnitTests/SolveJacTests.cpp finite-differences `residual` under IDA's
  convention (J = dF/dY + cj dF/dY') and checks that the linear solve satisfies
  J dy = g. With nScalars = 0 this passes at 3e-10, so solveHDGJac's static
  condensation is verified. With nScalars = 3 (ScalarTestLD3) it comes out O(1):
  ~0.3 in the DG field rows, 5-8 in the scalar rows.

  The error is NOT confined to the scalar rows, so it is either
    (a) the Woodbury/bordered elimination in solveJacEq, or
    (b) ScalarTestLD3::ScalarGPrimeExtended disagreeing with its own
        ScalarGExtended, which would corrupt the whole bordered solve.

  PIDTest cannot distinguish these: a wrong Jacobian only slows Newton, it still
  converges to the right answer, so the reference comparison stays green either
  way.

  To settle it: add a minimal TransportSystem with nScalars = 1 and
  G = mu - const, whose Jacobian is exactly known, and run the same J dy = g
  check. If that passes, the elimination is sound and the fault is in
  ScalarTestLD3's hand-written scalar Jacobian; if it fails, solveJacEq is
  wrong. Then re-enable the strict assertion in
  solve_jac_eq_handles_global_scalars (it currently asserts only finiteness and
  prints the residuals). See Tests/README.md.
